    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Can you give some specific examples of foods that fall into each category? I want to make sure the distinction is clear for the client. Also, why does the body respond so much faster to the high-GI ones during digestion?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure, high-GI foods often include white bread, sugary cereals, and fruit juices, while brown rice, non-starchy vegetables, and most fruits count as low-GI food options. The body responds faster to high-GI foods because they are digested and absorbed into the bloodstream quickly, prompting a rapid and substantial release of insulin to deal with the blood sugar surge. This process can create a cycle of highs and lows in blood sugar and subsequently in mood and energy levels.",
      "source": "target",
